.TH YAKK 1 "2026-08-21" "yakkity 0.2.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
yakk \- search, resume, hand off, and orchestrate coding-agent sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yakk
.RI [ option ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yakk
searches local Claude Code, Codex, Cursor Agent, and Grok sessions.
It can resume a selected session in its original agent, pass sanitized context
to a different agent, or start a background worker through Herdr.
.PP
Only user-visible user and assistant prose is transferred between agents.
System records, hidden reasoning, attachments, tool calls, and tool results are
excluded.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -l ", " --list
List the ten most recent conversations.
.TP
.BI --list N
List the
.I N
most recent conversations. The number is appended directly to the option,
for example
.BR --list25 .
.TP
.BR --off ", " -off
Select a conversation and start a different agent with compact sanitized
context. The beginning and recent end are retained when the transcript is
large.
.TP
.BR --ass ", " -ass
Select a conversation and start a different agent with the full sanitized
transcript. Yakk asks for confirmation above an estimated 50,000 input tokens.
.TP
.BI --spawn-herdr " --agent agent --task text"
Start the requested worker agent in a background Herdr tab with compact
sanitized context from the supervising agent's current conversation. This
command is non-interactive and must run inside a Herdr-managed pane. The
worker is started with its agent's own non-interactive/auto-approve flag, so
it never sits waiting at a permission prompt with nobody to answer it.
.TP
.B --workers
List workers started by Yakk with each one's live state
.RB ( pending ", " working ", " blocked ", " ready ", or " malformed )
and whether its sanitized context still exists
.RB ( present " or " expired ).
.TP
.BI --collect " name"
Print a ready worker's structured result to standard output. A supervising
agent can run this command to bring the result into its current tool context.
Refuses to print a
.B blocked
or
.B malformed
result and explains why instead.
.TP
.BI --retarget " name --agent agent"
Resubmit an existing worker's stored task and sanitized context to a
different agent, without redescribing the task. Starts a new worker; the
original worker's Herdr tab is left running, untouched, for separate
inspection. Fails if the original worker's context has already expired (see
.BR YAKK_WORKER_CONTEXT_TTL ).
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Show concise command help.
.TP
.BR -v ", " --version
Show the installed version.
.SH WORKER ORCHESTRATION
Yakk's transcript parser creates the sanitized context, records each worker,
and retrieves its result. Herdr creates a background tab, starts the
requested agent with that agent's own non-interactive/auto-approve flag, sends
the task, and reports lifecycle state.
.PP
Each worker agent is resolved through a single adapter table mapping the
requested agent name to its Herdr
.BR --kind ,
its destination command, and its non-interactive flag. Switching a worker's
agent, including via
.BR --retarget ,
never requires redescribing the task \(em only the adapter lookup changes.
.PP
.B --workers
and
.B --collect
report one of five live states, combining Herdr's own agent status with a
structural check of the result file:
.TP
.B pending
The worker has not started producing output yet.
.TP
.B working
The worker is actively running.
.TP
.B blocked
The worker is stuck at an approval prompt despite its auto-approve flag.
Inspect its Herdr pane and approve manually, or fix the adapter's flag for
that agent.
.TP
.B malformed
The worker wrote a result file, but it does not contain the requested
Outcome / Files changed / Verification / Unresolved issues headings.
.B --collect
refuses to print it.
.TP
.B ready
The result file matches the expected shape and
.B --collect
will print it.
.PP
A supervising agent should use this loop:
.IP 1. 3
Confirm that an edit-capable worker will run in its own Git worktree and
branch.
.IP 2. 3
Run
.B --spawn-herdr
with
.B --agent AGENT --task TEXT
and one concrete, bounded task.
.IP 3. 3
Continue useful supervisor work instead of continuously polling the worker.
.IP 4. 3
Run
.B yakk --workers
when status is needed.
.IP 5. 3
Run
.B yakk --collect
with the reported worker name once its state is
.BR ready .
.IP 6. 3
Inspect the worker branch, diff, and verification before merging or pushing.
.PP
The preview does not create Git worktrees automatically. The worker inherits
the supervising session's working directory. Never allow multiple
edit-capable agents to use the same checkout concurrently.
.PP
A worker's sanitized context file is deleted by Yakk itself, not by
instructing the worker to delete it, once it is older than
.BR YAKK_WORKER_CONTEXT_TTL .
This is deliberate: a worker's context has to still exist after
.B --collect
succeeds for
.B --retarget
to resubmit the same task to a different agent later.
.PP
Worker output is untrusted input. Verify important claims and repository state
locally before taking integration or release actions.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HERDR_ENV
Must equal 1 for
.B --spawn-herdr
to run.
.TP
.B HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID
Identifies the Herdr workspace where Yakk creates the worker tab.
.TP
.B YAKK_STATE_DIR
Overrides the private directory used for worker metadata and result files.
The default is a user-specific directory beneath the operating system's
temporary directory.
.TP
.B YAKK_WORKER_CONTEXT_TTL
Seconds a worker's sanitized context file survives before Yakk deletes it.
Default 86400 (24 hours); 0 disables expiry. Worker metadata and results are
never affected.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Selects the temporary-directory root used for handoffs and worker state.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $YAKK_STATE_DIR/workers/name.json
Private worker metadata, including agent kind, Herdr tab and pane identifiers,
source session, task, result path, and context file path.
.TP
.I $YAKK_STATE_DIR/workers/name.md
Structured worker result written for
.B --collect.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Zsh, fzf 0.74.3 or newer, jq, a running Herdr session, and at least one
supported coding-agent CLI, installed and authenticated.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. One indicates a missing dependency, unavailable
session or worker result, failed Herdr operation, or other runtime failure.
Two indicates invalid command usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
yakk
yakk --off
yakk --spawn-herdr --agent claude --task "Review the installer"
yakk --workers
yakk --collect yakk-claude-1724260000-12345
yakk --retarget yakk-claude-1724260000-12345 --agent codex
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-worktree (1),
.BR zsh (1)
